+**Also consult when the risk warrants it** (ambiguous requirement, security/data/infra impact,
+production deploy, missing or flaky test coverage):
+- Requirement: is the understood requirement complete/ambiguous?
+- Testing: suggest edge cases or review coverage.
+- Deploy: sanity-check the release/rollout plan.
+- Maintain: a periodic second read on what to optimize/improve.
+
+**Skip** for trivial one-off questions, pure investigation, and small changes the diff or tests
+already verify (roughly: a few dozen lines, docs/config moves, renames). A change that touches
+security, infra, or data is never "small": consult.
+
+**Keep each consult cheap:**
+- One consult per gate: batch all questions into one prompt, send to zcode and agy in parallel.
+- Ask for a short answer on stdout ("reply in <=30 lines"); use a file only when it must be longer.
+- Pass the diff or a path, not a pasted transcript of the session.
+- Give them only what the question needs: no secrets, credentials, or customer/PII data
+  (e.g. eVisa). Whatever they read goes to an external model provider.
+
+Claude still drives every stage and makes the decisions. Use the `/zcode` and `/agy` skills to
+invoke them.
+
+## Delegate mechanical work to Claude subagents
+
+Delegation only saves tokens when it *replaces* work Claude would otherwise do inside its own
+context. Hand these mechanical sub-tasks to a Claude subagent (Explore / context-finder: isolated
+context, returns a summary) and read only the summary. Decisions and the final verification stay
+with Claude:
+- reading/summarizing large files, logs, or docs
+- broad code search
+- first drafts of boilerplate
+- long test/lint/build loops
+
+zcode/agy are not delegates for this: the SDLC rule above says no external tool does Claude's work
+in its place.
+
+## Keep sessions short
+
+Every turn re-reads the whole context, so cost grows with session length. One task per session;
+when the context passes about 150k tokens, wrap up, write a handoff, and continue in a fresh
+session.
